        <section class="guide-page" id="start-here" data-page="2" aria-labelledby="start-here-label"><div class="page-label" id="start-here-label">Section 2 of 9</div>
            <h2>Getting Started</h2><h3>Initial Setup</h3>
            <p>Audio Router requires a companion audio driver and helper service. When you launch the app for the first time after extracting it from the downloaded &quot;.dmg&quot; file, the Setup panel opens automatically. Select &quot;Install Driver&quot; to install the required driver and helper and finish setting up Audio Router.</p>
            <h3>Reinstall or repair the driver</h3>
            <p>Audio Router relies on its bundled audio driver and helper service. If either component is unavailable, or the shared configuration folder cannot be accessed, the app automatically opens the Setup panel to help you reinstall the driver or repair the helper service.</p>
            <ol class="steps"><li><strong>Choose Install Driver or Repair Driver.</strong> Complete the macOS Installer window. Administrator approval may be required.</li><li><strong>Return to Audio Router.</strong> Wait while the app finishes setup. If prompted, choose <em>Check Again</em> after a moment.</li><li><strong>Retry if necessary.</strong> Use the setup panel to open the install log when the installer completes but the helper still does not load.</li></ol>
            <aside class="note warning"><strong>Important</strong> Closing the main Audio Router window quits the app. Allow each update to finish before closing.</aside>
            <h3>Create your first session</h3>
            <ol class="steps"><li><strong>Add a session.</strong> Click the large <strong>+</strong> button at the bottom of the sidebar (Newly created sessions automatically have the pass-through device added).</li><li><strong>Select it.</strong> Newly created Audio Router device will be selected automatically. Its row is highlighted and its volume control appears.</li><li><strong>Name it.</strong> If you like to rename it, click the pencil on the selected row. Enter your desired name and press Return or click elsewhere to finish.</li><li><strong>Enable it.</strong> Newly created session will be enabled by default. Use the power button to toggle it. A green indicator means enabled; a red indicator means disabled.</li>
            </ol>
            <aside class="note info"><strong>Tip</strong> Create separate sessions for recording, streaming, conferencing, or a dedicated application mix. Each session keeps its own routing graph.</aside>
            <h3>Workspace orientation</h3>
            <ul><li>Colored blocks represent input sources, the Audio Router channel matrix, and monitor devices.</li><li>Small circles on block edges are ports. Red curves are active wires.</li><li>Horizontal level meters show live signal activity; green is lower and yellow/orange/red is progressively hotter.</li><li>The sliders/settings icon in a block header opens that node’s options panel.</li></ul>
            <a class="back" href="#top">Back to index ↑</a>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-page" id="how-audio-router-works" data-page="3" aria-labelledby="how-audio-router-works-label">
            <div class="page-label" id="how-audio-router-works-label">Section 3 of 9</div>
            <h2>How Audio Router works</h2>
            <p>Audio Router presents each configuration as a <strong>session</strong>. A session contains one virtual Audio Router device output, one or more input sources, a channel matrix, optional monitor devices, and the wires connecting them. </p>
            <p>Sessions can be configured with pass-through device or with application and hardware input sources. If a session is configured with a pass-through device, it will not allow any other sources to be added. If a session is configured with application or hardware input sources, the pass-through device will not be available.</p>
            <p>Remove pass-through to add application or hardware input sources, and remove all application and hardware sources to add pass-through device.</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Session sidebar</td><td>Create, select, rename, enable, mute, and set the input level of sessions.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Input Sources</td><td>Audio-producing apps, hardware inputs, or a Pass-Through source.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Output / Channels</td><td>The virtual output channel matrix. Channels are added or removed in stereo pairs.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Routing Wires</td><td>Routes audio from the input sources to the output channels to optional monitor devices.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Monitor</td><td>A physical output used to hear the routed mix.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Bottom toolbar</td><td>Add or remove sources, channel pairs, wires, and monitor devices.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
            <aside class="note info"><strong>Signal flow</strong> Audio travels left to right: Input Sources → Channels → Monitor. A wire maps one source port to destination ports.</aside>
            <a class="back" href="#top">Back to index ↑</a>
        </section>
<section class="guide-page" id="build-a-route" data-page="4" aria-labelledby="build-a-route-label"><div class="page-label" id="build-a-route-label">Section 4 of 9</div>
<h2>Build a route</h2><h3>Add input sources</h3>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Select a session.</strong> Source controls are unavailable until a session is selected.</li><li><strong>Open Sources.</strong> Click <span class="fw-bold">(+)</span> button beside Sources. The list refreshes when the popover opens.</li><li><strong>Choose a source.</strong> Select a running app, an available hardware input, or Pass-Through.</li></ol>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Source type</th><th>Use it when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application</td><td>Capture audio produced by a specific app, such as Safari, Music etc.</td></tr><tr><td>Hardware input</td><td>Add microphone, line, or interface input channels to the mix.</td></tr><tr><td>Pass-Through</td><td>Make the Audio Router channels follow a pass-through device/source directly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<aside class="note warning"><strong>Source rule</strong> Pass-Through cannot coexist with application or hardware capture sources. Remove Pass-Through before adding capture sources; once capture sources exist, Pass-Through device can not be added.</aside>
<h3>Connect ports</h3>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Start at a port.</strong> Drag from a circular port on a source or destination block.</li><li><strong>Drop on a compatible port.</strong> Release when the target port highlights or snaps. A red wire appears.</li><li><strong>Repeat per channel.</strong> Wire left/right or individual hardware channels to the required output channels.</li></ol>
<p>When a new input source is added, wires are automatically created to connect it to the output channels. To remove a wire, click the line, when it's selected then click <strong>Remove Wire</strong>. Clicking empty canvas clears the selection.</p>
<h3>Manage output channels</h3><p>Use <strong>(+)</strong> and <strong>(−)</strong> beside Channels to add or remove stereo pairs. A session has at least 2 channels and supports up to 32. Removing a pair also removes connections that use those ports.</p>
<h3>Add a monitor device</h3>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Click (+) beside Monitor.</strong> Choose an available output device. The popover shows its output-channel count.</li><li><strong>Connect Channels to Monitor.</strong> Ports are auto connected between the Audio Router device output channels and the monitor device. Connections can be deleted or recreated as draging from Channels output ports to monitor input ports.</li><li><strong>Listen and meter.</strong> Start playback and confirm movement in the source, channel, and monitor meters.</li></ol>
<a class="back" href="#top">Back to index ↑</a>
</section>
<section class="guide-page" id="controls-reference" data-page="5" aria-labelledby="controls-reference-label"><div class="page-label" id="controls-reference-label">Section 5 of 9</div>
<h2>Controls reference</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sidebar row</td><td>Select a session. Click on pen icon to rename it.</td></tr><tr><td>Power icon</td><td>Enable or disable that session.</td></tr><tr><td>Microphone/speaker icon</td><td>Mute or unmute the selected session or node.</td></tr><tr><td>Volume slider</td><td>Set level from 0.00 to 1.00.</td></tr><tr><td>Sidebar (+) / (−)</td><td>Create a session or remove the selected session (up to 64 sessions).</td></tr><tr><td>Sources (+) / (−)</td><td>Add a source or remove the selected source block.</td></tr><tr><td>Channels (+) / (−)</td><td>Add or remove a stereo output pair (2–32 channels).</td></tr><tr><td>Remove Wire</td><td>Delete the selected connection.</td></tr><tr><td>Monitor (+) / (−)</td><td>Add a monitor or remove the selected monitor block.</td></tr><tr><td>Block options icon</td><td>Open or close the options panel for that node.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Input Node options</h3><ul><li>Source and monitor nodes: adjust input/output or monitor volume and mute state.</li><li>Application sources: use <strong>Mute when capturing</strong> to control whether original app output is muted while Audio Router captures it.</li></ul>
<h3>Selection behavior</h3><ul><li>Select a source or monitor block before using its toolbar (−) button.</li><li>Select a wire before Remove Wire becomes available.</li><li>Unavailable controls are dimmed. Hover over one to see its action or reason.</li></ul>
<h3>Reading status and meters</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Green running dot</td><td>The session is enabled and its audio device is running.</td></tr><tr><td>Gray dot</td><td>The session is not running, or it is disabled.</td></tr><tr><td>Moving source meter</td><td>Audio is reaching the source node.</td></tr><tr><td>Moving channel meter</td><td>Signal is reaching that Audio Router channel.</td></tr><tr><td>Moving monitor meter</td><td>Signal is reaching the physical monitor device.</td></tr><tr><td>Unavailable overlay</td><td>The app or hardware source is no longer available; reopen/reconnect or replace it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<a class="back" href="#top">Back to index ↑</a></section><section class="guide-page" id="pass-through-routing" data-page="6" aria-labelledby="pass-through-routing-label"><div class="page-label" id="pass-through-routing-label">Section 6 of 9</div>
<h2>Pass-Through routing</h2><h3>Workflow A — Pass-Through routing</h3>
<figure><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Pass-Through source wired one-to-one into a two-channel Audio Router output"><figcaption>Pass-Through source wired one-to-one into a two-channel Audio Router output.</figcaption></figure>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Create or select a session.</strong></li><li><strong>Add Pass-Through.</strong> Choose it from Sources while the session has no capture sources.</li><li><strong>Map channels.</strong> Connect/Route Channels as you prefer.</li><li><strong>Adjust level if needed.</strong> Open the Pass-Through options from its block header.</li></ol>
<aside class="note info"><strong>Expected result</strong> The source and Channels meters move together. Add a monitor only if you also want to hear the result through a physical output.</aside>
<a class="back" href="#top">Back to index ↑</a></section><section class="guide-page" id="app-microphone-routing" data-page="7" aria-labelledby="app-microphone-routing-label"><div class="page-label" id="app-microphone-routing-label">Section 7 of 9</div>
<h2>App and microphone routing</h2><h3>Workflow B — Mix an app and microphone</h3>
<figure><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Safari and external microphone feeding two Audio Router channels and external headphones"><figcaption>Safari and an external microphone feeding the two-channel matrix, with external headphones as monitor.</figcaption></figure>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Add the application.</strong> Click Sources <span class="fw-bold">(+)</span> and choose the required running app &quot;Only running apps will be shown in the list.&quot; from the popup list (To be able to add a application, remove pass-through device if it's present).</li><li><strong>Add the microphone.</strong> Open Sources again and choose the external microphone or audio interface.</li><li><strong>Map the app.</strong> Connect/Route the app’s left/right outputs to Channels.</li><li><strong>Map the microphone.</strong> Connect its channel to the required output channel or channels. Multiple sources can contribute to the channel mix.</li><li><strong>Add headphones.</strong> Click Monitor <span class="fw-bold">(+)</span>, choose the speaker, headphone device etc., and connect Channels 1/2 to its left/right inputs.</li><li><strong>Balance the mix.</strong> Set gain/mute in each source’s options. Avoid sustained orange/red meter levels.</li></ol>
<aside class="note danger"><strong>Feedback caution</strong> When routing a live microphone, use headphones and begin at a low monitor level. Avoid speakers the microphone can hear.</aside>
<h3>Workflow C — Expand beyond stereo</h3>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Add channel pairs.</strong> Use Channels <span class="fw-bold">(+)</span> until the matrix has the number of outputs you need.</li><li><strong>Wire intentionally.</strong> Map each source channel to the desired numbered output. Use channel labels identify the ports.</li><li><strong>Match the destination.</strong> Choose a monitor or downstream app/device capable of using those channels.</li></ol>
<a class="back" href="#top">Back to index ↑</a></section><section class="guide-page" id="troubleshooting" data-page="8" aria-labelledby="troubleshooting-label"><div class="page-label" id="troubleshooting-label">Section 8 of 9</div>
<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Checks and corrective action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No Audio Router device appears</td><td>Complete driver setup. Choose Check Again. Reinstall the driver and inspect the install log if the helper does not respond.</td></tr><tr><td>Source <span class="fw-bold">(+)</span> button is disabled</td><td>Select a session. Remove Pass-Through before adding app/hardware sources. Resolve an invalid mixed configuration.</td></tr><tr><td>An app is missing from Sources</td><td>Start audio playback, reopen Sources to refresh it, and verify the app is running.</td></tr><tr><td>Hardware input is missing</td><td>Reconnect or power on the device, verify macOS sees it, then reopen Sources.</td></tr><tr><td>No sound at Audio Router output</td><td>Enable and unmute the session; raise levels; verify wires reach the correct Channels ports; check meters.</td></tr><tr><td>No sound in headphones/speakers</td><td>Add the device under Monitor, wire Channels to it, unmute, and raise monitor volume.</td></tr><tr><td>Meters move only at the source</td><td>The source-to-Channels wire is missing, mapped incorrectly, or the destination is muted.</td></tr><tr><td>A block is unavailable</td><td>Restart/reconnect its app or device. Remove and re-add the node if needed.</td></tr><tr><td>Remove button is disabled</td><td>Select the matching source, monitor, session, or wire first.</td></tr><tr><td>Installer finished but setup remains open</td><td>Wait briefly and choose Check Again. Open the install log and run Reinstall Driver if necessary.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Safe reset sequence</h3>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Stop playback.</strong> Lower monitor volume and stop microphone monitoring.</li><li><strong>Disable the session.</strong> Use the sidebar power icon.</li><li><strong>Simplify the graph.</strong> Keep one source, two Channels, and one monitor; recreate the wires.</li><li><strong>Test left to right.</strong> Confirm source, Channels, then monitor activity before adding more sources.</li><li><strong>Re-enable and expand.</strong> Add remaining nodes one at a time.</li></ol>
<a class="back" href="#top">Back to index ↑</a></section><section class="guide-page" id="subscription-help-removal" data-page="9" aria-labelledby="subscription-help-removal-label"><div class="page-label" id="subscription-help-removal-label">Section 9 of 9</div>
<h2>Subscription, help, and uninstallation</h2><h3>Free mode and subscription</h3>
<p>In free mode, consuming audio trough Audio Router longer than 10 minutes include noise. A subscription unlocks unlimited clean routing. Use <strong>Help → Subscription…</strong> to activate a license, refresh or restore status, or open billing management. Checkout and billing are handled through Lemon Squeezy.</p>
<aside class="note info"><strong>License activation</strong> Use the license key from your Lemon Squeezy receipt. An internet connection may be needed to confirm or refresh status.</aside>
<h3>Cancel a subscription</h3>
<p>You can cancel future subscription renewals at any time through Lemon Squeezy:</p>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Subscription.</strong> In Audio Router, choose <strong>Help → Subscription…</strong>.</li><li><strong>Open Billing.</strong> Click <strong>Billing</strong> to open the Audio Router billing portal.</li><li><strong>Sign in.</strong> Enter the email address used at checkout and follow the secure sign-in link sent by Lemon Squeezy.</li><li><strong>Cancel renewal.</strong> Open the active subscription, choose <strong>Cancel subscription</strong>, and confirm.</li></ol>
<p>You can also open the <a href="https://audiorouter.lemonsqueezy.com/billing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Audio Router billing portal</a> directly.</p>
<aside class="note warning"><strong>Cancellation timing</strong> Cancellation prevents the next renewal. Premium access remains active until the end of the current paid billing period. Uninstalling Audio Router or removing its license key does not cancel the subscription.</aside>
<p>When the paid period ends, Audio Router updates the license during its periodic check. To refresh immediately, choose <strong>Help → Subscription…</strong> and click <strong>Check License</strong>.</p>
<h3>Help and support</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Menu command</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Help → User Guide</td><td>Opens the online help site. Shortcut: Command-?.</td></tr><tr><td>Help → Contact Support</td><td>Starts an email to <a  href="#" id="email-link">&#115;&#117;&#112;&#112;&#111;&#114;&#116;&#64;&#99;&#104;&#97;&#116;&#116;&#121;&#115;&#112;&#97;&#114;&#114;&#111;&#119;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;</a></td></tr><tr><td>Audio Router → About Audio Router</td><td>Shows the app version and build number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Uninstall Audio Router</h3><p>Choose <strong>Help → Uninstall Audio Router</strong>, review the warning, and confirm. This removes the driver, helper LaunchAgent, and shared AudioRouter support files then delete the &quot;Audio Router.app&quot; from application folder.</p>
<aside class="note danger"><strong>Before uninstalling</strong> Stop active consumers and routing sessions. Uninstalling is a system-level change and may require administrator approval.</aside>
<h3>Quick success checklist</h3><ul><li>The driver and helper are installed and responding.</li><li>A session is selected, enabled, and unmuted.</li><li>Every source has a wire to the intended Channels port.</li><li>Channels has the required number of output pairs.</li><li>A monitor is added and wired if local listening is required.</li><li>Meters show signal from left to right without sustained clipping.</li></ul>
